# Chapter 13. The I/O Page

One page, $D000-$DFFF, always visible. The authoritative register-level
reference is the machine's own headers, and Appendix A, The Registers is
generated from them at build time -- the way the Neo6502 handbook generates
its keyword reference from the firmware source. This chapter is the map of
what lives where:

=> A1-REGISTERS.GMI Appendix A, The Registers

```
$D000  VICKY     video: layers, palette, blitter, sprites, SHEILA
$D100  input     keyboard FIFO and its status, the held keys, the mouse,
                 break-pending
$D200  DMA       copy / fill / swap / line / triangle
$D300  storage   the host filesystem: open, read, dir, chdir...
$D480  OPL2      the YM3812: address, data, status -- the AdLib's arrangement
$D500  system    clock, RTC, frame counter, DUMP, the settings the ROM reads,
                 the sound sequencer at $D5E0
$D600  banks     the eight bank registers, masks at $D620
$D700  MATH      IEEE floats, transcendentals, math lists, mul/div
$D800  Tube      the co-processor: status, byte in, byte out, start/stop
$D900  N:        the network: four channels, tcp://, http(s):// and tnfs://
                 (see core/net.h)
$DA00  JIM       the terminal: a VT100/ANSI in hardware, drawing on the
                 console's screen (see core/term.h)
$DF00  far gate  call slots, descriptor table pointer, depth
```

## A split screen: SHEILA

The Apple II put four lines of text under its graphics; C64 programmers did
the same with a raster interrupt timed to the line. On this machine the CPU
does nothing at all: SHEILA, VICKY's display-list coprocessor (the Amiga's
copper is its ancestor), runs a short list from memory at every frame, in step
with the raster, and a register it writes takes effect on the line about to be
drawn. A split is six instructions:

```
MOVE 10,00     line 0: layer 0, the console's text, off
MOVE 20,19     layer 1, a 640-wide 8 bpp bitmap, on
WAIT 416         26 text rows of 16 lines: the split
MOVE 20,00     the bitmap off
MOVE 10,07     the text on: rows 26-29 are text
END              and again from the top, next frame
```

With the status bands up, the split has three parts rather than two: the text
layer stays on at line 0 for the top band, the bitmap takes over at the band's
end, and the text comes back for the last four rows and the bottom band --
eight instructions. SPLIT reads where those lines fall from the console itself
($DA06, its rows, and $DA08, the rows above it), so it is right with the bands
up or down. Each instruction is four bytes -- the operation (1 WAIT, 2 MOVE, 0
END), then its operands -- and the list is anywhere in memory, a 28-bit
address in $D060-$D063, started by 1 in $D064. A MOVE is exactly a write by
the CPU, so any VICKY register can change mid-frame: the mode, the scroll, a
layer, the palette. Two rules: line numbers count the glass, 0-479, whatever
the mode (in a 240-line mode each line is drawn twice, so split on an even
one); and nothing turns SHEILA off after a program, so a program that turns it
on turns it off when it leaves.

=> IMG/SPLIT.PIC SPLIT

SPLIT: blitter lines above, the console's last four rows below, and SHEILA
holding the line between them.

SPLIT is the demonstration, and it has two twins that draw the same ribbon of
lines -- endpoints bouncing, the line sixteen behind erased -- as fast as they
can, and say how fast in the text band: /LANG/EHBASIC/EX/SPLIT.BAS, with
EhBASIC's own LINE. At the 40.5 MHz a host starts at:

```
Language   lines/s  how it draws one
C (SPLIT)  7400     fifteen register writes
EhBASIC    330      LINE, one blitter operation
```

The blitter draws a line in no time at all, so the numbers are the languages:
what a BASIC spends is its own arithmetic, its arrays and its line lookups,
and three hundred lines a second is a good deal more than a game of 1985 asked
for. The split itself costs every one of them nothing. A faster clock (F12, or
SETUP) scales all three.

